---- 1. xcodebuild build ----------\necho \"--- [1/4] xcodebuild build ---\"\nif [ ! -d \"$REPO_ROOT/SunnyWalker.xcodeproj\" ] && [ ! -d \"$REPO_ROOT/SunnyWalker.xcworkspace\" ]; then\n  echo \"!! No Xcode project found. Skipping build.\"\n  BUILD_RC=99\nelse\n  xcodebuild \\\n    -scheme \"$SCHEME\" \\\n    -destination \"$DESTINATION\" \\\n    -derivedDataPath \"$DERIVED\" \\\n    -quiet \\\n    clean build 2>&1 | tee \"$REPO_ROOT/orchestrator/logs/$(date +%F)/_build.log\"\n  BUILD_RC=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\nfi\necho \"build rc=$BUILD_RC\"\necho \"\"\n\n# ---------- 2. xcodebuild test ----------\necho \"--- [2/4] xcodebuild test ---\"\nif [ $BUILD_RC -ne 0 ]; then\n  echo \"!! Build failed, skipping tests.\"\n  TEST_RC=99\nelse\n  xcodebuild \\\n    -scheme \"$SCHEME\" \\\n    -destination \"$DESTINATION\" \\\n    -derivedDataPath \"$DERIVED\" \\\n    -quiet \\\n    test 2>&1 | tee \"$REPO_ROOT/orchestrator/logs/$(date +%F)/_test.log\"\n  TEST_RC=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\nfi\necho \"test rc=$TEST_RC\"\necho \"\"\n\n# ---------- 3. SwiftLint ----------\necho \"--- [3/4] swiftlint ---\"\nif command -v swiftlint >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  swiftlint --quiet 2>&1 | tee \"$REPO_ROOT/orchestrator/logs/$(date +%F)/_lint.log\"\n  LINT_RC=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\nelse\n  echo \"!! swiftlint not installed (brew install swiftlint), skipping.\"\n  LINT_RC=99\nfi\necho \"lint rc=$LINT_RC\"\necho \"\"\n\necho \"=== validate.sh done at $(date -Iseconds) ===\"\necho \"summary: pbxproj=$PBXPROJ_RC build=$BUILD_RC test=$TEST_RC lint=$LINT_RC\"\n\n# exit with worst of the four (but 99=skipped doesn't fail)\nWORST=0\nfor rc in $PBXPROJ_RC $BUILD_RC $TEST_RC $LINT_RC; do\n  if [ \"$rc\" -ne 0 ] && [ \"$rc\" -ne 99 ]; then\n    WORST=$rc\n  fi\ndone\nexit $WORST\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012VUoxLPmHRgrg16iDSrUbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have everything I need to write the Day 4 D reviewer entry. Let me assess:\n\n**Alignment with spec:**\n\n1. **Day 4 milestone**: Per the spec's development schedule (Week 2, Day 10-11: SpeechRecognizer, console print; Day 12: wire AlarmRingView flow), Day 4 of this accelerated schedule roughly maps to getting AudioPlayer, SpeechRecognizer stub, AlarmRingView flow, and RewardView. D's Day 3 brief specified exactly these items. All were delivered.\n\n2. **Aesthetic/UX**: RewardView uses `GhibliColors.wheatGold`, `GhibliFonts.title(40)`, `GhibliColors.forestDeep` — all theme tokens. \n\nAlarmRingView uses scene gradients, CloudBackground, TotoroAvatar with wiggle animation, and no raw color literals.\n\n3. **On-device only**: AudioPlayer wraps AVAudioPlayer natively. SpeechRecognizer is a stub with no network calls or third-party SDKs.\n\n**Code quality spot-check:**\n\nAudioPlayer is structured as a final class with proper MainActor annotation and ObservableObject conformance for SwiftUI integration. The AVAudioPlayer wrapper handles playback without delegate callbacks currently wired up.\n\nSpeechRecognizer is a pure stub matching the spec interface.  `@Published` properties for `recognizedText` and `matchedKeyword` are in place, and the `startListening(onMatch:)` and `stop()` methods have the right signatures for Day 5 to drop in the real implementation without interface changes. It's marked `@MainActor final` correctly and imports the Speech framework as needed.\n\nIn AlarmRingView, the audio player and speech recognizer are properly owned as `@StateObject` instances. The audio fallback chain handles recording first, then falls back to a bundle sound file, then skips if neither works — which aligns with the spec intent. One thing to watch: if no alarm is passed to the view (like in previews), it defaults to \"totoro_breath.caf\", which could be a problem if that file doesn't exist.\n\nThe 5-second delay before speech recognition starts matches the spec requirement. The callback-based approach for `startListening` is fine for now since the stub won't actually trigger it — only the button tap will call `handleWakeUp()`. However, there's a threading concern: when the real SpeechRecognizer implementation runs on Day 5, the callback could fire on a background thread, and since `SpeechRecognizer` is `@MainActor`, I need to ensure the callback dispatches back to the main thread properly.\n\nThe RewardView correctly auto-dismisses after 3 seconds using a SwiftUI async task, which triggers the `onDismiss` callback that dismisses the AlarmRingView and returns to HomeView. One edge case to watch: if `handleWakeUp()` gets called multiple times (like if the user taps the button while the stub is still running), I should call `stopListening()` first to avoid double-calls to `stop()` on Day 5. The theme tokens are being applied correctly throughout.\n\nFor the RewardView specifics, the Totoro avatar displays as specified, and the confetti animation is intentionally deferred to Day 5. The reward shows for both voice and button tap paths, which aligns with the spec's intent even though it mentions voice specifically. Now checking the validation script — it's using null-safe iteration to find Swift files in the project...\n\nWriting validation logic... C followed through cleanly on the handoff, fixing all three files across the pbxproj sections and re-validating to confirm the changes stuck. Now looking at what's blocking Day 5: SpeechRecognizer is still just a stub and needs the real implementation wired up with on-device recognition enabled, audio assets are missing so playback won't work on actual devices, ConfettiSwiftUI got deferred, and there are a few lingering test gaps and state management issues from earlier days that haven't been resolved yet.\n\nThe build is solid—all 16 tests pass and the code is clean—but we're at 90% completion because the core speech recognition piece is incomplete and the polish work (confetti, audio assets) is still pending. Day 5 needs to focus on wiring up the real SpeechRecognizer with AVAudioEngine and on-device recognition, adding confetti effects to the RewardView, and getting those audio assets in place so everything actually runs on device. The real SpeechRecognizer implementation is the most critical piece, followed by AudioRecorder so parents can actually record custom wake phrases. I'm noticing the AudioPlayer doesn't implement AVAudioPlayerDelegate, which means isPlaying won't auto-reset when playback ends naturally—but since AlarmRingView always loops, this isn't blocking. There's also a potential issue where startListening gets called unconditionally after 5 seconds in AlarmRingView, even if handleWakeUp was already triggered.\n\nThe timing of view dismissal is tricky here. When showingReward becomes true, the fullScreenCover might keep the underlying AlarmRingView technically \"appeared\" until the reward is dismissed and dismiss() is called. This means the 5-second task could still fire after the view is gone, and with a real SpeechRecognizer implementation, that could cause problems—I should add a guard to prevent starting listening on a dismissed view.\n\nThere's also the issue that the stub's startListening doesn't throw, but the real implementation wraps audioEngine.start() in try, so Day 5 will need proper error handling there. For now, I'll note this as a known limitation and move on to writing the review. Let me identify the real issues:\n\n1. `AudioPlayer` doesn't set `player?.delegate = self`, so `isPlaying` won't auto-reset for non-looping playback. Minor for current use, but a design gap.\n2. `AlarmRingView` `.onAppear` Task: the 5-second delay + startListening runs even if `handleWakeUp()` was already called. With the stub this is harmless; with Day 5's real SFSpeechRecognizer, this could crash or produce unexpected behavior (starting AVAudioEngine after the view is in its dismiss sequence).\n3. `SpeechRecognizer.startListening` is not marked `throws` in the stub, but the spec's real implementation requires `throws` (AVAudioEngine.start() throws). Day 5 will need to either add `throws` to the interface or catch internally — this is an interface mismatch risk.\n\nThese are the most substantive technical issues I see. Now I'm adding the D entry to ring.md.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2130,"cache_read_input_tokens":47387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2130,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d86cd2a7-e909-402c-aeb7-c0f920b07704","uuid":"4c5f8cd5-fff4-47b2-bcb6-0690b78f3f11","request_id":"req_011CbaKfSe87TwvTY3VZnJc2"}
